  <page>
    <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Calculate the following limit:
            <md>
                \lim_{x\rightarrow 0}\frac{e^{2x}-1}{\sin(x)}
            </md>
        </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
        <p>
            Note that <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow} e^{2x}-1 = 0</m>, and <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 0}\sin(x) = 0</m>, and so we can apply L'Hospital's rule.
            Thus
            <md>
                <mrow> \lim_{x\rightarrow 0}\frac{e^{2x}-1}{\sin(x)}\amp \overset{\text{L'Hospital}}{=} \lim_{x\rightarrow 0}\frac{\frac{d}{dx}(e^{2x}-1)}{\frac{d}{dx}\sin(x)} </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp= \lim_{x\rightarrow 0}\frac{2e^{2x}}{\cos(x)} </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp= \frac{2e^{0}}{\cos(0)}=\frac{2}{1}=2. </mrow>
            </md>
            
        </p>
    </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Calculate the following limit:
                <md>
                    \lim_{x\rightarrow 2}\frac{x^2-x-2}{x-2}
                </md>

            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                Note that <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 2}x^2 -x -2 = 0</m>, and <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 2}x-2=0</m>.
                Thus, we can apply L'Hospital's rule to calculate the limit.
                <md>
                    <mrow> \lim_{x\rightarrow 2}\frac{x^2-x-2}{x-2} \amp\overset{\text{L'Hospital}}{=} \lim_{x\rightarrow 0}\frac{\frac{d}{dx}(x^2-x-2)}{\frac{d}{dx}(x-2)} </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp=\lim_{x\rightarrow 2} \frac{2x-1}{1}</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp= \frac{2(2)-1}{1} = 3. </mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>

        <solution>
            <p>
                We can also answers this question without resorting to L'Hospital's rule.
                Note that <m>x^2-x-2 = (x+1)(x-2)</m>.
                Thus,
                <md>
                    <mrow>\lim_{x\rightarrow 2}\frac{x^2 -x -2}{x-2}  \amp =\lim_{x\rightarrow 2}\frac{(x+1)(x-2)}{x-2}</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp= \lim_{x\rightarrow 2}(x+1) = 3. </mrow>
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find all the critical numbers for the function
                <md>
                    f(x) = x^{3}-12x + 20
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                The critical numbers occur where <m>f'(x)=0</m> or <m>f'(x)</m> is undefined.
                We have that
                <md>
                    f'(x) = 3x^2 - 12.
                </md>
                The derivative <m>f'</m> is defined everywhere, thus to find the critical numbers we set <m>f'(x)=0.</m>
                Solving <m>2x^2-12 -0</m> we have
                <md>
                    <mrow>3x^2 \amp= 12</mrow>
                    <mrow>x^2 \amp = 4 \text{ (dividing both sides by <m>3</m>)}</mrow>
                </md>
                Thus, the critical numbers of <m>f(x)</m> are <m>x =2</m> and <m>x=-2.</m>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>